\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132377 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132377 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47ba82cc-1df6-5020-89db-060fe72e8652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132378 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1^{2} & 1^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2^{2} & 2^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3^{2} & 3^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & 4^{2} & 4^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5^{2} & 5^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132378 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132378 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5aa074ec-7f5b-5875-8e08-d39bd51ac885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133615 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133615 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](images/fe/fe5d2bbb69e110d08d3231d486af483dc05d995e30907a5f9a62cf46743e81d7.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98ec57bb-04f5-559a-9057-854de19eda7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133615 #edb/ref 1074 "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186133616 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133616 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n![](images/03/0329ad2c30d00d6051e2be094d076960d3ecfc35f9054feac195787f3af7328d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5354fe5-fa4b-5443-a097-40174b0b7b16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133616 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\nX \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133635 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133635 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $40$ | $70$ | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](images/93/93a2e368924ac5d892ce203e2b2c2ee0007ebb30bb71476272385d5b26695113.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133635 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75c0086b-db74-5f01-8d66-f023dd7b575d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133635 #edb/ref 1074 "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** | **$\\color{blue}6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}70$ | $\\color{red}120$ | $\\color{red}100$ | $\\color{red}80$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ 70 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 100 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Design Matrix When the Regression Curve Contains Zero Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133806 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7b2c90d8-0f2e-5f84-bf67-7620d6f1d308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Expression for a Least-Squares Regression Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133807 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133823 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1045 "Polynomial Regression With Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134030 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8d26a5ff-3bd1-560e-af9c-1dfbf5779d4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134389 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb11277b-38b1-5736-b659-e34045c52325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134706 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134706 #edb/ref 1061 20311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134706 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5282b6c3-a052-5524-8cfc-ee4738c1759c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134706 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134707 #edb/ref 1061 20312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d8abdce-a161-520c-ae2e-5c81f7417b17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134708 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134708 #edb/ref 1061 20313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8359737f-d4ca-50ac-871a-c58906d6165e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134734 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134734 #edb/ref 1061 22549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91936449-d1f1-5123-8737-4025e640187f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134890 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134890 #edb/ref 1061 37335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134890 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e556ba45-b685-5183-8ad9-099c86720b9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134890 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135063 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nIn particular, this would mean that the sum of the squared residuals [math]\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2[/math] must be as small as possible.\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nNow, using the matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the condition that this sum must be minimized is equivalent to minimizing [math]\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2[/math] or, which is the same, minimizing [math]\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.[/math]\n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135063 #edb/ref 1065 3654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135063 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a9d76d67-952b-5a5c-8891-757974db3a7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135063 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135144 #edb/ref 1064 "In some cases, we might need to constrain the regression curve so that some of its coefficients equal zero. \n\nConsider the table below, which gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units. \n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $8$ | $18$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x=0.$ The company wishes to find a *quadratic* curve that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0).$\n\nSo, our regression curve must pass through the point $(0,0).$ In other words, the $y$-intercept of the quadratic curve must be $0.$ Therefore, we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/0f/0f98711850478ba6a152103452446e1b2a97ca64c2d47ab7c257db2d05335fcf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=5 \\\\ 3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=8 \\\\ 4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135144 #edb/ref 1065 6152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135144 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "34af7736-1458-54b5-b831-80b4e22a03b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135144 #edb/ref 1067 "Regression Curves Containing Zero Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266166 #edb/ref 1080 "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}Xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266166 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266166 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02bf34ad-5a8f-589b-8414-32c9c540d88f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266174 #edb/ref 1080 "X{X}^{T}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266174 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266174 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0367ed7d-e350-54f1-a687-13952716b56d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266269 #edb/ref 1080 "(X{X}^{T})^{-1}Xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266269 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266269 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ebbcf4c-85fa-5ae4-a906-8d02b96e424b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266336 #edb/ref 1080 "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266336 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266336 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1750cd69-1431-5ce9-b1f9-19feb9e4912b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266381 #edb/ref 1080 "(X{X}^{T})^{-1}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bdaf9d4-dc08-5b00-a67d-9a8c62aa29f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266605 #edb/ref 1080 "X({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34a7084c-c30f-5232-858b-9de95f8dc7d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266612 #edb/ref 1080 "{X}^{T}\\,(X{X}^{T})^{-1}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266612 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266612 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "362a79c7-8381-508d-a693-01e846420040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266644 #edb/ref 1080 "(X{X}^{T})^{-1}y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266644 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266644 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39348ecb-402a-5f85-b880-7dce810e3a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759